          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "**Urgency: the tool returned EMERGENCY.** Its rule matched the words \"chest pain\" in the screening list, not a symptom you described. You have not told me about chest pain. Still, the rule's action is: call emergency services now if you have chest discomfort, because it can mean a heart attack. Do not drive yourself. (Source: red-flag-chest-pain)\n\n**Check for these now. If any apply, call emergency services immediately:**\n- Chest pain or discomfort, or breathlessness with it (red-flag-chest-pain; red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty).\n- A sudden, severe headache, especially the \"worst of your life\" kind that peaks within about a minute, or a headache with new confusion, drowsiness, weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, or visual loss (red-flag-sudden-severe-headache).\n- Shortness of breath at rest, or breathlessness that comes on suddenly or stops you speaking in full sentences (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty).\n- New vision changes, weakness or numbness on one side, or difficulty speaking. The hypertension guidance says a very high reading with these is an emergency, not a routine appointment (condition-hypertension).\n\n**If none of these apply:** The retrieved guidance does not give a threshold for a reading of 155/95 without symptoms, so these sources cannot tell you how urgent that reading is alone. Contact your doctor or another clinician the same day to report the readings and ask whether a review is needed. Do not wait for a routine appointment if the readings keep rising or you feel unwell.\n\n**Treatment and follow-up:** Treatment is chosen by risk level and combined with lifestyle measures: less salt, more vegetables, fruit and whole grains, regular activity, and not smoking. One retrieved passage (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*) describes monthly review until the target is reached, then review every three to six months once readings are stable, plus checks of kidney function and potassium after starting certain medicines. That passage is written for people starting or changing treatment, so it may not apply to you. Another review said the drug classes, targets and follow-up intervals were not in the notes it retrieved. The retrieved passages do not say when to restart treatment after stopping. Your prescriber should decide whether to restart, which medicine, and what dose. Do not change anything on your own.\n\n**Classification:** Under US office-reading thresholds (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*), 140/90 or higher is stage 2. These thresholds are for office readings, and no thresholds for home readings were found. US and European guidance also differ: US guidance treats 130/80 as the start of hypertension, while European guidance starts at 140/90 and treats 130–139 / 80–89 as high-normal. The same reading can therefore be labelled differently depending on which guidance is used, and the bodies differ on how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension.\n\n**How strong is the evidence?** The retrieved passages do not grade the strength of evidence behind these recommendations, so this answer cannot say how well supported any of them is.\n\n**Next step:** Contact the clinician who prescribed your tablets today, tell them your readings, and tell them you stopped treatment two months ago. Ask them whether and when to restart, how often to check your readings, and what readings should prompt a call.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
